			<div class="content" id="post-content-4684157">
									

<p>As a contest entry&#8230;</p>
<p>As an entry in the Exquisite Corpse contest, this is a remarkable combined effort. I appreciate how incredibly complex and creative <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/maliceaforethought" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2902864); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2902864&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1591846168" alt="MaliceAforethought" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2902864)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/maliceaforethought" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2902864); return false;">MaliceAforethought</a></span>'s SCProcs are (they're certainly the most involved Procedures I've come across thus far of the entries), and how the majority of it is seamlessly implemented in the article itself.</p>
<p>I've been eager to see how authors would adapt to working in the constraints of purely writing SCProcs, and this perfectly showcases the level of attention and effort I was looking forward to in this contest. It's also fitting that the tall order of this prompt is filled by a narrative which progressively gets more wild as it incorporates its many seemingly-disparate elements.</p>
<p>If I had any complaints, it would be that it doesn't seem like much of the second paragraph (SCProcs) made their way into the main article. While it's certainly still a significant portion of the overall SCProcs, it could be that I'm missing how it's inferred somewhere.</p>
<p>In all, if I were voting solely on articles' merit as contest entries, this would have my upvote.</p>
<p>Of course, this did have my upvote, but that's the subject of another post.</p>

